  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe 500MB ridge over the central CONUS will shift east as \nnorthwest flow sets up over the Great Lakes and into the Upper\nOhio Valley. This will set up a series of shortwaves to travel\nsoutheast over the region the next few days. The timing of the\nshortwaves over the next few days will be the main issue as hi-\nres models and ensembles are not handling the setup very well \nin the timing and placement. \n\nThere is a conditional potential for severe storms on Tuesday.\nML CAPE increases to between 2000 and 3000 J/kg by late\nafternoon and into the evening but consensus in the models\nsuggests a more overnight threat and MU CAPE aloft suggests a\ngood 2000 to 3000 J/Kg CAPE overnight if the instability can be\ntapped into during the overnight hours. \n\nWednesday appears to be a day where the threat will be more in\nthe late afternoon and evening. Current indications are the \nridge should begin to break down late Wednesday and Thursday, as\na shortwave and surface cold front approaching. The potential \nfor severe storms will also increase by Wednesday evening, \nincreasing again on Thursday as convection increases in diurnal \nheating along the slow moving front. Increasing NW flow aloft \nshould result in increased shear and diurnal instability. The \npotential continues for severe storms into Thursday. The latest \nML guidance indicates the increasing severe potential, \nespecially late Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nRising 500mb heights with the building ridge should result in \nwarmer temperatures each day through mid week, with the heat \npeaking Wednesday when the ridge is strongest across the Upper \nOhio Valley region. Most locations outside of the higher terrain\nshould reach the 85 to 90 degree range today, though there is a\ncontinued potential for convection and/or patchy cloud cover \nwhich could affect daytime highs. The heat should peak \nWednesday, as highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s for most \nareas. The combination of heat and humidity should result in \nheat index values in the mid to upper 90s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
